course: everyday
lesson: 108
title: Laundry machines, the freezer and electricity at home
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Electricity flows easily through water and through your body, which is why wet hands, water on the floor and plugs never go together.
explain: Damaged cables, hot plugs and chargers smothered under bedding are warning signs of a fault that can start a fire.
explain: Washers, dryers and freezers are safe when used properly, but a child can be trapped inside a machine, and frozen metal can stick to wet skin.
explain: For example, in the UK and Ireland bathrooms have pull-cord light switches, so that wet hands never touch an ordinary switch near the bath.
fact: Never let anyone climb into a washing machine, tumble dryer or old fridge; children can be trapped and suffocate, which means they cannot breathe.
fact: A washing machine that shakes and walks across the floor should be stopped and a grown-up told.
fact: The tumble dryer's lint filter should be cleaned after each use, because fluff can start a fire.
fact: Keep away from plugs and switches if there is water on the floor, and tell a grown-up.
fact: Wet skin freezes and sticks to frozen metal, tearing when you pull away, so never put your tongue on it.
fact: Liquid expands when it freezes, so a can left in the freezer can burst.
fact: Dry your hands before touching plugs or switches, because water conducts electricity into your body.
fact: Never use mains electrical things near a full bath or sink, because a device falling in can electrocute the person in the water.
fact: Never touch or use a frayed cable with wires showing; tell a grown-up.
fact: A hot plug or socket, or a smell of burning, means an electrical fault: switch off at the wall if safe and tell a grown-up at once.
fact: Charge phones and tablets on a hard surface, never under a pillow, because chargers get hot.
fact: In a power cut, use a torch (flashlight) rather than candles, and keep the fridge closed so the food stays cold for hours.
fact: The UK and Ireland use three square pins, the USA and Canada two flat pins, and Australia and New Zealand slanted pins, so travellers need a proper adaptor.
exercise: Your little brother wants to hide inside the tumble dryer. What do you do? | Stop him and keep the doors shut | Let him, if the door stays open | Let him, but only for a short time | Close the door so nobody finds him
exercise: Why is a washing machine or dryer a dangerous hiding place? | A child can be trapped and run out of air | The drum is too cold inside | It might start a new wash all by itself with the door shut | It is full of soap that stings
exercise: The washing machine is shaking and moving across the floor. What do you do? | Stop it and tell a grown-up | Sit on it to hold it still | Push it back while it spins | Turn it up to a faster spin
exercise: Why clean the fluff from the tumble dryer filter? | Built-up fluff can start a fire | Fluff makes clothes smell of soap | Fluff makes the dryer too quiet | Fluff turns the clothes grey
exercise: There is water on the floor around the washing machine. What do you do? | Keep away from plugs and switches and tell a grown-up | Unplug the machine with your wet hands | Mop it while touching the switch | Turn the machine off at the socket while standing barefoot in the water
exercise: You want to lick a frozen metal ice tray. What is true? | Wet skin freezes and sticks to it | Frozen metal is safe to lick | Only plastic sticks to your tongue | Your tongue warms it at once
exercise: Why should a can of soda not be left in the freezer for long? | It freezes, expands and can burst | It will turn into a different drink | It gets fizzier and tastes better | The can will melt in the cold
exercise: Your hands are wet and you want to plug in the hair dryer. What do you do? | Dry your hands first | Plug it in quickly | Use only one wet hand | Hold the plug with a wet towel
exercise: Why should a hair dryer never be used near a full bath? | If it falls in, it can electrocute the person in the water | The steam from the bath will get inside and break the hair dryer | It will make the bath go cold | The noise will scare people
exercise: A cable has a split with wires showing. What should you do? | Not touch or use it, and tell a grown-up | Wrap it with sticky tape and use it as normal | Use it just once more | Twist the wires back together
exercise: A plug feels hot and smells of burning. What do you do? | Switch off at the wall if safe and tell a grown-up at once | Leave it, plugs are often warm | Pour a glass of water on the plug to cool it down quickly | Cover it with a cloth
exercise: Where should you charge your tablet at night? | On a hard surface like a desk | Under your pillow | Under the duvet to keep it warm | On the bed next to you
exercise: There is a power cut in the evening. What is safest for light? | A torch | Candles in every room | A lighter held in your hand | Matches struck one after another
exercise: Why keep the fridge shut in a power cut? | The cold stays inside and the food keeps for hours | The fridge will restart faster | It stops the fridge leaking water | Food goes bad faster in a closed fridge, so open it to let air in
exercise: A family from New York goes on holiday to London. What do they need for their chargers? | A proper travel adaptor, as UK plugs have three square pins | Nothing at all, because plugs and sockets are the same all over the world | To push their plugs in hard | To cut the plug off and use bare wires
test: Lucas in Ireland finds a puddle near the washing machine plug. Which is the safest action? | Keep away from the plug and call a grown-up | Pull the plug out with damp hands | Stand in the puddle and switch it off quickly at the plug | Wipe the socket with a wet cloth
test: Why does water make electricity more dangerous? | Water conducts electricity into your body | Water stops electricity completely | Water makes plugs colder and safer | Electricity cannot pass through people, only through wires
test: Why are UK bathroom light switches often pull-cords? | So wet hands never touch an ordinary switch | Because they are cheaper to make | So children cannot reach them | Because pull-cords give more light than ordinary switches
test: A girl in Alaska touches her wet tongue to a frozen metal pole. What happens? | Her tongue freezes to it and tears if she pulls | Her warm tongue heats the pole and she is perfectly fine | Nothing happens to wet skin in the cold | The pole melts where she touches it
test: Why is charging a phone under the pillow risky? | The charger gets hot and the bedding can catch fire | The phone charges too slowly | The pillow drains the battery | The phone might ring in the night and wake you up
test: Which is the best way to light a room during a power cut? | Use a torch | Light candles near the curtains | Turn on the gas hob | Use a lighter for an hour
test: An old fridge is left in the garden with its door on. What is the danger? | A child could climb in and be trapped | It could fill with rainwater | It will start to cool the whole garden down in summer | It will attract only birds
test: A bottle of water is forgotten in the freezer overnight. What is likely? | It froze, grew bigger and may have cracked | It turned into steam inside the bottle overnight | It became smaller and lighter | Nothing at all happened to it
test: Which sign shows an electrical fault you should report at once? | A plug that is hot and smells of burning | A lamp that is switched off | A socket on the wall with nothing plugged into it | A cable that is coiled neatly
test: lil bro wants to hide in the tumble dryer for hide n seek lol | No, he could get trapped and not breathe, stop him | Fine as long as the door stays open a little bit | Fine if it's switched off | Fine for a short game only
test: hands still wet from washing, just gonna flick the light on | Dry your hands first, water carries electricity | It's fine, light switches are always safe to touch | Only wet feet matter, not hands | Use your elbow and it's fine
test: put a can of fizzy in the freezer 2 cool it fast, forgot it overnight | It may burst, because liquid gets bigger when it freezes | It will be perfectly fine, just nice and extra cold | It will shrink and go flat | It turns back into water in the morning
test: power's out, can i light candles in my room | Use a torch instead and keep the fridge shut | Yes, light lots of them so you can see properly | Yes, but leave them on the bed | Open the fridge for its light
explain: A boy in Leeds pushed a wet finger near a socket to get a plug out; he got a shock that threw his hand back and left a burn on his finger.
explain: A girl in Ohio climbed into the dryer during hide-and-seek; the door shut and she could not open it from inside, and luckily her mum heard her knocking.
fact: Water and electricity together can give a dangerous shock, so you never touch switches or plugs with wet hands.
fact: Never touch or climb near a power line, even to get a kite back; power lines carry enough electricity to kill, so tell an adult.
fact: Children have been trapped in washing machines, dryers and old freezers that cannot be opened from inside.
test: hands wet, just gonna flick the switch | Dry your hands first, water and electricity can shock you | Flick it fast, it's fine if you're quick | Use your elbow wet | Only plugs are dangerous wet
test: hide n seek, gonna hide in the dryer | No, you can get trapped and not breathe well, hide somewhere safe | Yes, it's the best spot, nobody ever looks there | Yes, if you leave a sock in the door | Only hide there for a minute
test: kite stuck on a power line, gonna get it with a stick | Leave it and tell an adult, power lines can kill | Climb up the pole and grab it, it's not far | Use a wet stick | Throw stones at it
test: did u hear a kid hid in the dryer playing hide n seek n got locked in, they say he was in there two whole days. what's probably exaggerated | Two days; the danger is real, kids can be trapped and suffocate | That a child got into a dryer at all | That the door cannot be opened from inside | Nothing, kids hide in dryers safely all the time and it is a really good spot
test: Charlie put his tongue on the freezer tray for a dare n it stuck n half his tongue came off, the year 6s say. what's the real lesson | Wet skin sticks to frozen metal, so never put your tongue on it | Only the freezer door sticks, the tray is safe | Pull your tongue off fast, because that stops it sticking | Frozen metal is only dangerous in summer
test: Ivy's phone charger set her pillow on fire at night n her room burnt, my mate says it's cos Ivy's family are messy. should i repeat that | No, that bit is unkind; the lesson is charge on a hard surface | Yes, because messy families deserve to have everyone know about it | Yes, but only to people who know Ivy | Yes, so people stop visiting her house
exam: Identify the principal reason for which plugs and switches must not be touched with wet hands. | Water conducts electricity into the body | Water causes the plug to fall from the socket | Wet hands cause the bulb to flicker only | Water causes the switch to rust at once
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a plug that is hot or a smell of burning near a socket? | A sign of an electrical fault requiring prompt attention | A normal effect of using electricity during the winter months | A sign that the device is charging quickly | A harmless effect of dust upon the plug
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a can of liquid placed within a freezer. | The liquid expands upon freezing and the can may burst | The liquid contracts upon freezing and the can is crushed inwards | The can is preserved without change indefinitely | The can becomes electrified by the cold metal
exam: Pursuant to a failure of the electrical supply, ascertain the advisable course of action. | Use a torch and keep the refrigerator closed | Light candles in every room of the house | Open the refrigerator so the food stays fresh | Unplug the refrigerator and leave it open
